  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">STRENGTHENING LOCAL COMMUNITY ACTION TO CONSERVE JAGUARS IN THE GREATER LACANDONA ECOSYSTEM, MEXICO.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Jaguars now occupy just 50% of their historical range, with most of their populations being threatened by retaliatory killing in response of jaguar predation upon livestock, habitat destruction and prey depletion. This project is intended to improve the coexistence between jaguars and the local communities in the Greater Lacandona Ecosystem, Mexico, by mitigating the jaguar predation upon domestic livestock through the improving of husbandry practices by local ranchers and protecting the most vulnerable animals to jaguar predation implementing electric fences and night enclosures. Further, this project will build a sound conservation strategy for jaguars in the Southern Mayan Forest of Mexico. Specific activities include: (1) capacity building of livestock owners and technification of ranches to reduce predation risk by jaguars; (2) create a robust community-based monitoring program focused on jaguars and their prey in Protected Areas and jaguars corridors; (3) outreach activities aimed to children and adults; (4) identification with key stakeholders the priorities actions to mitigate the threats for jaguars inthe Greater Lacandona Ecosystem, and (6) develop a bi-national (Mexico and Guatemala) action plan for jaguar conservation in the Southern Mayan Forest.</narrative>
  </description>
